                    "comment": "np.where - choose between two arrays by a mask"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 922,
                    "position_y": 26,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1016,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Builds a new array cell by cell: where the condition is true it takes the value from 'if true', elsewhere from 'if false'.\n\nThink of it as a stencil or a masked blend rather than a lookup. Feed it a comparison as the condition and you have \"use this where the reading is good and that where it is not\" across a whole array in one step - no loop, no branching. Replace bad readings with a fallback, paste one image into another through a mask, zero everything below a threshold.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.where\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ncondition:\nThe mask. Usually the result of a comparison node (>, <, ==) on an array, but any array works: a non-zero value counts as true. This is the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nif true / if false:\nThe values to choose from. Each can be an array the same shape as the condition, or a single number used everywhere - 'if false' 0 blanks every cell the condition rejects. These inlets are cold: they are stored, and used when the next condition arrives. Send them FIRST.\n\nThe node sends NOTHING until both 'if true' and 'if false' have received something. A np.where with one branch unwired stays silent and gives no sign why. If the shapes cannot be matched up (a 10 x 10 condition with a 6 x 6 'if true'), nothing is sent either; with verbose on the console names the shapes.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nselected:\nAn array the shape of the condition, each cell from one source or the other.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_ramps lays a 10 x 10 square of points from -1 to 1 and sends three pictures of it, ten times a second. 'if true' is the x of each point (dark on the left, bright on the right); 'if false' is the y (dark at the top, bright at the bottom); the third is each point's distance from the centre. < 0.75 turns the distance into the condition: a white disc, true inside, black outside. np.where's result shows the left-to-right ramp inside the disc and the top-to-bottom ramp around it. Drag the number on the < node and the disc grows or shrinks.\n\nmake_ramps sends x and y before the distance, so both pictures reach np.where before the condition does - the order matters, because only the condition makes it send. Open make_ramps to see how.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.argwhere answers a different question about the same mask: not what values to use, but WHERE the true cells are. The comparison nodes (>, <, ...) make the condition. np.clip limits values without a mask. In PyTorch, t.masked_select (on the t.index_select page) takes out just the values where a mask is true. np.[] selects by position.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1000,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Builds a new array cell by cell: where the condition is true it takes the value from 'if true', elsewhere from 'if false'.\n\nThink of it as a stencil or a masked blend rather than a lookup. Feed it a comparison as the condition and you have \"use this where the reading is good and that where it is not\" across a whole array in one step - no loop, no branching. Replace bad readings with a fallback, paste one image into another through a mask, zero everything below a threshold.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.where\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ncondition:\nThe mask. Usually the result of a comparison node (>, <, ==) on an array, but any array works: a non-zero value counts as true. This is the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nif true / if false:\nThe values to choose from. Each can be an array the same shape as the condition, or a single number used everywhere - 'if false' 0 blanks every cell the condition rejects. These inlets are cold: they are stored, and used when the next condition arrives. Send them FIRST.\n\nThe node sends NOTHING until both 'if true' and 'if false' have received something. A np.where with one branch unwired stays silent and gives no sign why. If the shapes cannot be matched up (a 10 x 10 condition with a 6 x 6 'if true'), nothing is sent either; with verbose on the console names the shapes.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nselected:\nAn array the shape of the condition, each cell from one source or the other.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_ramps lays a 10 x 10 square of points from -1 to 1 and sends three pictures of it, ten times a second. 'if true' is the x of each point (dark on the left, bright on the right); 'if false' is the y (dark at the top, bright at the bottom); the third is each point's distance from the centre. < 0.75 turns the distance into the condition: a white disc, true inside, black outside. np.where's result shows the left-to-right ramp inside the disc and the top-to-bottom ramp around it. Drag the number on the < node and the disc grows or shrinks.\n\nmake_ramps sends x and y before the distance, so both pictures reach np.where before the condition does - the order matters, because only the condition makes it send. Open make_ramps to see how.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.argwhere answers a different question about the same mask: not what values to use, but WHERE the true cells are. The comparison nodes (>, <, ...) make the condition. np.clip limits values without a mask. In PyTorch, t.masked_select (on the t.index_select page) takes out just the values where a mask is true. np.[] selects by position."
                },
